#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cantor_staircase

#% text
# `cantor_staircase(x)` gives the Cantor function (the Cantor staircase) at `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The function is 0 for `x <= 0` and 1 for `x >= 1`, constant on each middle third removed in the construction of the Cantor set, continuous and nondecreasing.

#% item
# At rational `x` the value is exact: the ternary expansion of `x` is eventually periodic, and the periodic part sums as a geometric series. At inexact `x` it is computed to the precision of `x`.

#% item
# At other exact arguments it stays unevaluated; `n` evaluates it numerically.

#% item
# Lists are mapped elementwise.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
cantor_staircase(1/3)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
cantor_staircase(15/17)
#% output
#= 3/4

#% cell
cantor_staircase(0.76)
#% output
#= 0.6875

#% cell
n(cantor_staircase(1/sqrt(2)), 20)
#% output
#= 0.625000000000000000000000

#% cell
cantor_staircase([[1/2, 0], [0, 1/2]])
#% output
#= [[1/2, 0], [0, 1/2]]

